diagnosis_text:
A complete four-phase sweep of the dc_clos_bgp fabric found no fault. Phase 1 reachability confirmed 0% packet loss in both directions between pc_0_0 (10.0.0.2) and pc_0_1 (10.0.1.2). Phase A (infra_sweep + l2_snapshot) showed zero ACL/firewall rules, no static ARP poison, no duplicate MAC addresses, and all host addressing correct. Phase B (bgp_snapshot + tc_snapshot) confirmed all five FRR routers — both leaves, both spines, and the super-spine — have fully Established BGP sessions with no flags, no null/blackhole routes, and default fq_codel qdiscs (no shaping or corruption). Phase D (pressure_sweep) found no stress tools, no CPU pressure, and no socket spikes on any device.

The team's specific claim — "leaf-spine adjacencies keep bouncing" — was tested directly via `show bgp neighbors` on both leaf routers. Every leaf-spine session shows `Connections established: 1; dropped: 0` and `Notifications: 0 sent / 0 received`. Sessions have been continuously up since lab startup (~3 minutes). Interface counters on all leaf ports show zero carrier errors and zero dropped packets. There is no flap script or process anywhere in the lab, no daemon restart evidence, and no NOTIFICATION history that would indicate a past reset.

Root cause: There is no fault. The BGP fabric is operating correctly, all sessions are stable, and end-to-end host reachability is intact. The team's belief that adjacencies are bouncing is not supported by any current or historical evidence in the live network.

Fix: No corrective action is required. The reported symptom cannot be reproduced and no fault mechanism exists in the current lab state.
=== END ===